WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.240
CI/CD compromise can turn the build pipeline into a path for unauthorized payment

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.340
application changes. Source control permissions, runner environments, deployment tokens,

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.520
and artifact repositories all need protection.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:18.380
Unsigned code and unverified artifacts make it difficult to prove that deployed software

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.840
is authentic and unchanged.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.540
Integrity verification helps protect the path from source to build to release.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:30.960
Insecure deserialization occurs when applications trust serialized data to reconstruct

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.020
objects or behavior.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.720
Attackers may manipulate serialized input to alter state, bypass controls,

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.020
or trigger unsafe server behavior.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.880
Build pipeline attacks are especially relevant when deployment automation can affect

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.500
payment pages, scripts, APIs, or administrative tools.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:54.360
Change traceability, approvals, and monitoring help reduce this risk.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:57.540
Use code signing and artifact integrity checks.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.040
Secure CI/CD pipelines with least privilege and approvals.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:04.300
Monitor and log system and application changes.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:07.880
Selected PCI DSS mappings for this topic include Req.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:17.540
6.3 - Software integrity protections; Req. 11.5 - File integrity monitoring; Req.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.960
10.2 - Logging of system changes.
